🧠 1. Is a separate .class file generated for Lambda?
👉 ❌ NO
✔ Lambda does NOT create:
Separate .class file
Separate inner class
🔥 Example
Runnable r = () -> System.out.println("Hello");
👉 This does NOT create:
Test$1.class ❌


how works:
Lambda expressions are executed using JVM’s invokedynamic instruction,
and the implementation is created
dynamically at runtime instead of generating a separate .class file.
__________________________________
🔴 Anonymous Class
Runnable r = new Runnable() {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }
};

✔ What happens?
👉 Compiler creates:

Test.class
Test$1.class  ✅ (extra file)





+++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

🧠 Step-by-Step Understanding
🔹 1. Normal Java Code

When you write:

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }
}


👉 Compiler creates:

Test.class

🔹 2. Anonymous Class
Runnable r = new Runnable() {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }
};


👉 Compiler creates:

Test.class
Test$1.class  ✅ (extra file)

🔹 3. Lambda Expression
Runnable r = () -> System.out.println("Hello");


👉 Compiler creates:

Test.class ✅ ONLY


❌ No extra .class file
+++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
🔥 Behind the Scenes Example

Your code:

Runnable r = () -> System.out.println("Hello");


👉 Internally becomes something like:

private static void lambda$main$0() {
    System.out.println("Hello");
}


👉 JVM links it using:

invokedynamic

🎯 Flow Diagram
Lambda → Compiler → invokedynamic → JVM → runtime object → execute

🧠 Why Java uses this approach?
✅ Benefits:

No extra class file

Better performance

Less memory usage

Faster execution
++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

🚀 So Java introduced: invokedynamic

👉 It allows:

Dynamic method linking

Runtime decision

No need to create class file

+++++++++++++++++++++++++++
🧠 2. How Lambda uses invokedynamic

Your code:

Runnable r = () -> System.out.println("Hello");

🔥 What compiler does:

Instead of:
❌ creating class

It creates:
✔ a dynamic call instruction

🧠 JVM does this at runtime:

Sees invokedynamic

Calls LambdaMetafactory

Creates implementation

Returns object

🎯 Simple Flow
Lambda → invokedynamic → JVM → creates object → run()
+++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
🧠 3. Real-Life Analogy
Without invokedynamic (Old way):

👉 “Prepare full dish in advance”

With invokedynamic:

👉 “Cook only when customer orders”
++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
"LambdaMetafactory is an internal JVM class used to
create lambda implementations at runtime. It works with invokedynamic
to dynamically generate objects for
 functional interfaces without creating separate class files."

 ================================================
 "Lambda expressions in Java do not create a separate class
 file like anonymous classes. Instead, the compiler uses the
 invokedynamic instruction, and at runtime, the JVM uses
 LambdaMetafactory to dynamically create an implementation
 of the functional interface. This makes
  lambdas more efficient in terms of performance and memory."

  "Anonymous class creates a separate .class file at compile time,
   but lambda does not. Lambda uses invokedynamic and creates the
  object at runtime, so it is more lightweight and efficient."

  invokedynamic = “decide later”
  👉 LambdaMetafactory = “create object later”

  | Step              | Meaning               |
  | ----------------- | --------------------- |
  | invokedynamic     | “Prepare when needed” |
  | LambdaMetafactory | “Kitchen makes food”  |
  | Output            | “Food delivered”      |
"invokedynamic delays the execution decision,
and LambdaMetafactory creates the lambda object at runtime."

